340 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 80 0 R
/T (Reviewing the historical progression from classical substitution and transposition techniques to advanced number-theoretic public-key algorithms highlights a clear technological evolution. Classical systems focused primarily on the property of *diffusion* at the character level, attempting to obscure the structural dependencies of human language by applying geometric shifts and permutations. While these early methods laid the groundwork for modern cryptography, they ultimately fell short because they operated within small, linear key spaces that could be easily parsed and broken using basic statistical analysis.)
/E (Reviewing the historical progression from classical substitution and transposition techniques to advanced number-theoretic public-key algorithms highlights a clear technological evolution. Classical systems focused primarily on the property of *diffusion* at the character level, attempting to obscure the structural dependencies of human language by applying geometric shifts and permutations. While these early methods laid the groundwork for modern cryptography, they ultimately fell short because they operated within small, linear key spaces that could be easily parsed and broken using basic statistical analysis.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
341 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 80 0 R
/T (Modern cryptographic systems overcome these limitations by combining both approaches into hybrid architectures. Symmetric standards like AES use advanced substitution-permutation networks to encrypt high-volume data traffic with extreme efficiency. Concurrently, public-key systems like RSA use complex number-theoretic transformations to securely exchange the symmetric keys over open, unencrypted networks. By analyzing these algorithms through the lens of concept maps, security engineers can visualize how individual technical choices interact to protect the broader system, ensuring that data confidentiality, integrity, and availability remain secure against evolving cyber threats.)
/E (Modern cryptographic systems overcome these limitations by combining both approaches into hybrid architectures. Symmetric standards like AES use advanced substitution-permutation networks to encrypt high-volume data traffic with extreme efficiency. Concurrently, public-key systems like RSA use complex number-theoretic transformations to securely exchange the symmetric keys over open, unencrypted networks. By analyzing these algorithms through the lens of concept maps, security engineers can visualize how individual technical choices interact to protect the broader system, ensuring that data confidentiality, integrity, and availability remain secure against evolving cyber threats.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
