1120 0 obj
<<
/Title (In the discipline of network security, establishing a precise taxonomy of vulnerabilities is paramount to engineering robust defense mechanisms. Security systems are continuously exposed to systemic vulnerabilities that can be systematically categorized into Security Attacks, Threats, Risks, and Security Goals. A Security Attack refers to any deliberate action that compromises the security of information owned by an organization. An attack is an active execution of an exploit against a system. Conversely, a Security Threat represents a potential violation of security, which exists when there is a circumstance, capability, action, or event that could breach security and cause harm. Threats represent the theoretical possibilities of exploitation, whereas attacks are the real-world manifestations of those threats. Security Risk is the probabilistic measure of the likelihood that a specific threat will successfully exploit a vulnerability, multiplied by the systemic impact or loss incurred by the organization. Finally, Security Goals constitute the core foundational pillars of information security, formally designated as Confidentiality \(ensuring that information is accessible only to authorized entities\), Integrity \(guaranteeing that data remains unaltered during storage and transmission\), and Availability \(ensuring that authorized users have uninterrupted access to information resources and systems upon demand\).)
/Dest [10 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 976 0 R
/Next 1192 0 R
>>
endobj
