/Title (Modern asymmetric cryptography relies heavily on concrete number-theoretic principles. Unlike symmetric systems that use bit-level shuffling and substitution blocks, asymmetric configurations like RSA and Diffie-Hellman depend on the mathematical difficulty of solving specific problems, such as integer factorization and discrete logarithms. The core mathematical foundations of this infrastructure include the Modular Multiplicative Inverse, the Extended Euclidean Algorithm, Fermat's Little Theorem, Euler's Phi Function, and Euler's General Theorem.)
/Dest [19 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 980 0 R
>>
endobj
