1331 0 obj
<<
/Title (Determining which classical encryption scheme is more vulnerable requires analyzing the specific cryptanalytic techniques used to break them. Substitution techniques are fundamentally more vulnerable to traditional frequency analysis attacks, whereas transposition techniques are more vulnerable to structural geometric profiling \(anagramming\).)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1318 0 R
/Next 1343 0 R
>>
endobj
1332 0 obj
<<
/Title (However, from a modern cryptanalytic perspective, both techniques are dangerously weak when deployed in isolation. This operational fragility is exactly why modern symmetric encryptions, such as the Data Encryption Standard \(DES\) and the Advanced Encryption Standard \(AES\), avoid relying on either technique exclusively. Instead, they integrate both approaches into a unified structure called a Substitution-Permutation Network \(SPN\). In an SPN architecture, substitution layers \(implemented via S-boxes\) establish high *confusion*, ensuring that the mathematical relationship between the encryption key and the ciphertext remains as complex as possible. Concurrently, permutation layers \(implemented via bit-shuffling or MixColumns transformations\) create high *diffusion*, rapidly spreading the statistical structure of single plaintext elements across the entire width of the ciphertext block. By chaining these alternating layers together across multiple processing rounds, modern block ciphers achieve robust, mathematically sound security profiles.)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1318 0 R
/Prev 1343 0 R
>>
endobj
1333 0 obj
<<
/Title (Information Hiding Framework)
/Dest [18 0 R /Fit]
