1342 0 obj
<<
/Title (frequency metrics completely unchanged. If an adversary tracks the overall frequency distribution of a transposition ciphertext, the resulting histogram remains identical to standard English prose.)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1330 0 R
/Prev 1354 0 R
>>
endobj
1343 0 obj
<<
/Title (In a pure monoalphabetic substitution environment, the mapping between plain and cipher space is static. Consequently, cryptanalysis requires nothing more than calculating the relative percentages of characters in the ciphertext sample. An analyst can decipher the message without needing any context regarding the key's length or configuration. In contrast, breaking a transposition cipher requires a multi- step approach. First, the analyst must identify the cipher as a transposition system by verifying that its letter frequencies match standard language statistics. Once confirmed, the analyst must determine the exact block or column width, which is achieved by calculating the geometric distance between matching letter structures \(a process known as sliding or anagramming\). Because transposition ciphers thoroughly scramble sequential text dependencies, they are highly effective at breaking up common pairs and triplets of letters \(digrams and trigrams\). This makes them structurally more resilient against simple, single- character statistical tracking than standard substitution systems.)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1318 0 R
/Prev 1331 0 R
/Next 1332 0 R
>>
endobj
